The marketplace already has some frontend search work started. Your job is to review what exists, preserve what works, and implement the missing pieces below in a clean, staged way.

Existing / partially implemented context

The marketplace frontend already has, or may already have:

Search by author
Search by script/instrument name
Tag-based search
Tag inclusion filters
Tag exclusion filters
Some frontend state for selected included/excluded tags
Some Postgres persistence layer
Some server-side validation
Possibly an existing LLVM-based compile-validation flow
Possibly a Go serverless backend already connected to Postgres

Do not assume these are complete. Inspect the codebase first, then update only what is missing or incorrect.

Workflow 1: Search UI and tag filtering
1. Preserve included/excluded tag state

The tag search widget should allow the user to type/search/filter the visible list of tags, but that should not remove existing included/excluded tags from page state.

Meaning:

If a tag is already included, it remains included.
If a tag is already excluded, it remains excluded.
Typing in the tag search box only controls what tag options are displayed to the user.
It should not mutate the underlying inclusion/exclusion filter state unless the user explicitly adds/removes a tag.
2. Populate tag search from Postgres

Ensure the tag inclusion/exclusion widget is populated from the actual Postgres tags table.

The tags table should be the source of truth.

Do not hardcode tags in the frontend.

3. Author/name search modes

For both author search and name search, add/verify search modes:

Contains
Begins with / starts with
Exact match

Default mode should be:

contains

The backend query behavior should match the selected mode.

Workflow 2: Database indexes and persistence layer

Review the persistence layer and migrations.

Ensure indexes are appropriate for the marketplace search patterns:

Script/instrument name search
Author search
Tag inclusion
Tag exclusion
Tag relationships / join tables
Sorting if already supported or planned

Optimize for the best tradeoff between:

Fast marketplace reads/searches
Reasonable upload/insert/update performance
Low-cost Postgres/serverless operation

Do not over-index blindly. Add indexes intentionally based on actual query patterns.

Workflow 3: Serverless Google OAuth

Implement OAuth for the serverless backend.

Start with:

Google OAuth only

Other OAuth providers can come later.

Requirements:

Users can log in through Google.
Backend creates/recognizes marketplace accounts.
Authenticated requests use a non-expired token.
Upload endpoints require valid auth.
Keep the OAuth implementation clean enough to support more providers later, but do not implement other providers now.
Workflow 4: Desktop client login

Add login support to the actual Wavy8Script desktop client.

The client has two forms:

VST
Standalone

Login must be available in both.

Requirements:

User can authenticate from the desktop client.
The client stores/uses a valid non-expired token.
Upload button is only usable when logged in with valid auth.
Token expiration should be handled gracefully.
Workflow 5: Upload limits and roles

Implement account restrictions.

Roles/plans:

Standard user
5 uploads
Premium user
200 uploads
Must be actively subscribed
Creator role
Unlimited uploads
Separate from premium
Creator does not automatically imply premium

Important:

Creator is a separate account flag/role.
Premium is subscription/access-related.
Do not call this “admin.”
Do not merge creator and premium logic.
Workflow 6: Upload validation and server-side compile check

When a logged-in user clicks upload:

1. Run server-side validations

Validate things like:

Script length
Required metadata
Any existing marketplace constraints

Preserve existing validations if present.

2. Compile the uploaded script server-side

The server should validate that uploaded scripts compile.

The compile path should match the desktop app as closely as possible.

Architecture expectation:

The same C++ code used in the desktop app should be pulled into the Go backend using CMake.
It should compile to a static library.
The Go app should call it across a C API boundary.
The exact same script prelude/postlude used by the desktop app should be applied server-side.
LLVM compile validation should run on the server.

If this LLVM/server compile validation already exists, verify it is wired into upload correctly.

If it does not exist, implement it.

Only valid, compiling scripts should be accepted into the database.

Workflow 7: Insert real test data

Once auth, roles, restrictions, validation, and compile checks are in place:

Add valid test marketplace entries.
Ensure uploaded scripts are guaranteed to compile.
Use this data to test search, tag filters, upload limits, and download flow.
Workflow 8: Download flow

All marketplace scripts should be open source.

Do not implement encrypted/closed-source download logic.

Marketplace philosophy:

Subscription controls marketplace access.
Users are not paying individually for other people’s instruments/scripts.
The marketplace should be open, shared, community-oriented, and remixable.
Users should be able to inspect and iterate on each other’s ideas.
Storage format

Marketplace entries may be stored compressed.

Expected flow:

Uploaded script source is compressed/zipped.
It is stored in the database in an ASCII-safe compressed format.
On download, backend/client decompresses it.
Formatting/readability

After decompression, the code should be formatted into a readable standard style.

Goal:

Normalize indentation and whitespace.
Discourage obfuscated-looking source.
Make arbitrary user code as transparent and readable as possible.
Improve safety/debuggability if someone ever finds an exploit path.

This is not “linting” in the sense of rejecting stylistic issues. It is primarily formatting/normalization.

Suggested execution order

Break this into separate agentic workflows if needed:

Inspect current frontend/backend/database implementation.
Fix marketplace search UI state and search modes.
Wire tag widget to Postgres tags.
Review/add database indexes.
Implement Google OAuth on serverless backend.
Add desktop login for VST and standalone.
Implement role/plan upload restrictions: standard, premium, creator.
Wire upload validation and LLVM compile validation.
Insert valid test data.
Implement and test open-source download/decompress/format flow.
Run end-to-end tests.

Final result should support:

Search by author/name with selectable match modes.
Tag inclusion/exclusion from database-backed tags.
Correct indexes.
Google login.
Desktop client login.
Upload limits based on standard/premium/creator.
Server-side compile validation.
Open-source compressed source storage.
Download with decompression and readable formatting.